course: politics
lesson: 8
title: Taxes and local councils: bins, roads and schools
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Taxes are the money people and businesses must pay to the government, and they pay for the shared things everyone uses: schools, hospitals, roads, street lights, bins, libraries and police.
explain: Some tax hides in shop prices: in most US states sales tax is added at the till, so a $10 toy costs a bit more, while in the UK a tax called VAT is already inside the price on the label.
explain: Your local council is the government closest to your door: it empties the bins before the foxes have a party, fills potholes, lights the streets and runs parks and libraries, paid partly by council tax from every home.
explain: Money is always limited, so councils must choose: fix this road or that one, keep the library open on Saturdays or pay for care for old people; when money is short, people notice bins, potholes and closed libraries first.
explain: Big countries are split into parts with their own leaders, like US governors and Canadian or Australian premiers, and grown-ups argue at Sunday lunch about whether taxes should be lower so people keep more money, or higher so there are better services.
fact: Tax is money people and businesses must pay to the government; it pays for public services everyone can use.
fact: In most US states, sales tax is added at the till rather than shown on the label; it pays for things like roads, police and schools.
fact: In the UK, VAT (Value Added Tax) is already included in the price; on many things it is a fifth of the price, and most food and children's clothes have no VAT.
fact: A local council runs things like bin collections, street lights, parks and libraries; it is paid partly by council tax, which each home pays, and partly by the national government.
fact: Small roads are often looked after by the council and big roads by the region or national government; fixing them costs a lot, so councils choose which potholes to fill first.
fact: Budget cuts mean spending less than before; libraries, youth clubs and swimming pools are often on the list when a council has less money.
fact: A mayor leads a town or city; some mayors are mostly for ceremonies, while elected mayors, like those of London or New York, have real power over things like buses and police.
fact: Each US state has a governor; each Canadian province and each Australian state has a premier; they run things like schools and hospitals, while the national government runs things like the army.
fact: Council meetings are usually open to the public, and people can sometimes speak or ask questions; councillors are elected to the council and vote on things like parks and roads.
fact: Lower taxes leave people more of their own money; higher taxes can pay for more services; different parties choose different mixes.
fact: In many parts of the USA, schools are paid for partly by property tax on houses in the area, so richer areas can raise more; states and the national government add money to try to make it fairer.
fact: Some people say sales tax is fair because everyone pays when they spend; others say it hits poorer families harder because they spend most of what they have.
exercise: Mateo's $10 toy costs more at the till in Texas. Why? | Sales tax is added at the till, not shown on the label | The shop secretly charges extra to children who shop alone | The price went up while he was queuing | The label was printed with a mistake on it
exercise: Zara sees "includes VAT" on a receipt in Manchester. What is VAT? | A tax already included in the price | A discount for children under ten | A fee for using a plastic bag | A tip for the person at the till
exercise: On many things in the UK, how big a part of the price is VAT? | A fifth of the price | Half of the whole price | All of the price, doubled | One hundredth of the price
exercise: Which of these usually has no VAT in the UK? | Most food and children's clothes | Toys and video games | Televisions and phones | Bicycles and football boots
exercise: Noah's mum shouts, "We pay council tax for this!" about the bins. What is council tax? | Money each home pays to the local council | Money paid to the King every year to look after his castles | A fine for leaving bins out too long | A tax that only shops have to pay
exercise: What does a local council usually run? | Bins, street lights, parks and libraries | The army, the navy and the air force | The Supreme Court and the King's Speech | Television channels and newspapers
exercise: Aisha's dad shouts, "Who's meant to fix this road?" after a pothole. What is true? | Small roads are often the council's job, big roads the region's or nation's | Every single road in the country is fixed by the King's own staff, wherever it is | Roads are fixed by whoever drives on them most | Nobody is responsible for fixing any road
exercise: Hannah's library closes on Saturdays "due to budget cuts". But it's free! Why? | It is free to use but paid for by taxes, and the council has less money | The librarians decided they did not like working on Saturdays any more at all | Free things can never be closed, so someone must have broken the rules there | Books became too expensive, so the library has nothing left to lend on Saturdays
exercise: Dylan sees a mayor in a gold chain cutting a ribbon, and another talking about buses. Why the difference? | Some mayors are mostly ceremonial, others are elected with real powers | Mayors with gold chains are always the more powerful ones, with the most to say about buses | One of them must be pretending to be a mayor | Every mayor does exactly the same job
exercise: Ethan's cousin in Ontario talks about the premier. Who is that like in Ohio? | The governor, who leads the state | The president, who leads the whole country | The mayor of the biggest town in Ohio | The judge who leads the Supreme Court
exercise: What do states and provinces usually decide, compared with the national government? | Things like schools and hospitals, while the nation runs the army | Everything, while the national government does nothing at all except vote | Only the colours of road signs | Only the dates of the national holidays
exercise: Ruby speaks at a council meeting about a skate park. Was she allowed? | Yes, council meetings are usually open and people can sometimes speak | No, only councillors and their families may ever enter the meeting room at all | No, children are banned from all meetings | Yes, but only if she pays council tax
exercise: Who votes on things like new parks and roads at the council? | Councillors elected to the council | The King's governor-general | The Supreme Court justices | Any person who walks past
exercise: Uncle Rob says taxes are too high; Auntie Jen asks who pays for the hospital. What are they arguing about? | Lower taxes and more money kept, or higher taxes and more services | Whether hospitals should exist at all, or be closed down to save money | Whether gravy should count as a tax when you have it with Sunday lunch | Which of them pays more for Sunday lunch when the family eats out together
exercise: Why might Priya's American school have shiny laptops while a nearby school has old books? | Schools are partly paid by local property taxes, so richer areas raise more | The laptops were won in a football match | Teachers buy laptops with their own money | The government gives every school exactly the same amount, so the shiny school saved up for years
exercise: What do critics of sales tax say? | It hits poorer families harder, because they spend most of what they have | It is far too small for anyone to notice, so it raises no money at all | Only rich people ever pay it, because poorer people are let off at the till | It is added only to vegetables
test: Your American cousin says, "Bruh, the store's lowkey scamming me, the price went up at the register." What is really happening? | Sales tax is added at the till in most US states | The store doubles prices for kids who look like they have money | The price changes depending on the weather | Shops are allowed to make up prices
test: A London kid says, "The council's bare useless, the bins ain't been done in time, foxes are havin' a rave." ("Bare" means very.) Who is meant to collect the bins? | The local council, paid partly by council tax | The national army, paid by the King | The foxes, who are trained to tidy up | Each family must drive its own rubbish to the sea
test: What would happen if nobody paid any taxes at all? | Shared things like schools, roads and police would have no money | Everything would get cheaper and nothing else would change | The King would pay for everything himself out of the money in his own castles | Councils would print free money for bins
test: Grandad in Yorkshire says, "That pothole's a reet bummer, council should've sorted it." ("Reet" means right, or very.) Why might it not be fixed yet? | Fixing roads costs a lot, so councils choose which holes go first | The council is not allowed to touch roads at all, so it must wait for the army | Potholes fix themselves if you wait a year | The King must sign before any hole is filled
test: Your friend, being a bit dry, says, "VAT? It's not real, the price didn't change at the till." What's the truth? | In the UK VAT is already inside the price on the label | VAT is only added when you pay by card | VAT is a type of sweet sold in shops | VAT is only charged in Scotland
test: Mum says, rather formally, "His Worship the Mayor shall declare the playground open." What kind of mayor job is this? | A ceremonial duty, like cutting a ribbon | Running the national army | Choosing the next prime minister | Counting votes in a general election
test: A Canadian friend says, "Our premier's fixin' to change the school rules, eh." ("Fixin' to" means about to.) Is that a normal thing for a premier? | Yes, provinces run things like schools | No, only the King can change school rules | No, premiers only run the army | No, premiers are a type of football team
test: At Sunday lunch Uncle Rob goes, "Taxes are way too high, the government wastes it!" and Auntie Jen snaps, "Then who paid for your knee?" Which summary is fair to both? | Both want a good country; they disagree about how much tax and what it pays for | Uncle Rob is right and hospitals should close | Auntie Jen is right and taxes should be as high as possible, whatever they pay for | Taxes have nothing to do with hospitals
test: A video game shows the mayor fighting a war against another country. What should be put right? | Armies are run by the national government, not by mayors | Mayors run their own armies in real life | Wars are decided by councillors at the library | The game is right; mayors declare wars
test: A boy shouts at the council meeting, "Skate park now or I'm wrecking the bins!" What's wrong with his way of speaking up? | Threats and damage are wrong; giving reasons calmly is how you are heard | Nothing, threatening people is the best and fastest way to win anything you want | Children are not allowed to want a skate park or ask the council for anything | The bins belong to him because his family pays council tax, so wrecking them is fine
test: A Scottish uncle says, "Wee library's shut on Saturdays, budget cuts, it's pure dreich." ("Dreich" means grey and gloomy.) What does "budget cuts" mean? | The council is spending less money than before | The library has run out of books to lend | The library was closed because of the weather | The budget was cut into pieces with scissors
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Value Added Tax in the United Kingdom? | A tax included within the displayed price of many goods, frequently amounting to one-fifth of that price | A tax added only at the point of sale, always left out of the displayed price, as with US sales tax | A charge levied exclusively upon food and children's clothing, and upon no other goods whatsoever, at one-fifth of the price | A voluntary contribution made at the discretion of the purchaser
exam: Identify the principal source, other than grants from national government, from which local councils in England defray the cost of refuse collection, street lighting and libraries. | Council tax payable in respect of each dwelling | Fines imposed upon persons who fail to vote | Voluntary donations from members of the royal family | Sales of tickets to council meetings
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the leaders of subdivisions of federal states. | United States states are led by governors; Canadian provinces and Australian states by premiers | United States states are led by premiers; Canadian provinces and Australian states by governors appointed by the President | All such subdivisions are led by the national President | Such subdivisions possess no leaders of any kind
exam: It is given that schools in many parts of the United States are funded in part from local property taxation. Determine the probable consequence. | Schools in wealthier areas may command greater resources than those in poorer areas | All schools in the United States receive precisely identical sums of money, whatever the area | Schools in poorer areas invariably receive the greater sums, since property there costs less | Property taxation has no bearing whatsoever upon the funding of schools in any part of the country
